<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heidelberg Castle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> Castle is the ruined residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> palatine above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> in Heidelberg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Built and extended from the thirteenth century, it gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> palaces such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-henry-elector-palatine">Ottheinrich</a> Building, but French troops blew up much of it in 1689 and 1693 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a>, and a lightning fire in 1764 ended plans to restore it. Its ruins became a symbol of German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heidelberg-castle.jpg" alt="Heidelberg Castle" width="320" height="182" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Motatcho · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heidelberg-2726936.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1155 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> made his half-brother Conrad of Hohenstaufen Count Palatine, and the region became known as the Electoral Palatinate. The first mention of a castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> dates from 1214, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-duke-of-bavaria">Louis I, Duke of Bavaria</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a>, received it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> emperor Frederick II. A document of 1303 names two castles: an upper one on the Kleiner Gaisberg, destroyed by lightning in 1537, and a lower one on the Jettenbühl, the present site, founded sometime between 1294 and 1303.</p><p>When Ruprecht became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1401 the castle was so small that on his return from his coronation he had to camp in the Augustinians' monastery. He wanted more space for his court and guests and more defences, to make it a fortress.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The ruin stands about 80 metres up the northern Königstuhl hillside and dominates the view of the old town. It counts among the most important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> structures north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. The Friedrichsbau, built between 1601 and 1607 by the architect Johannes Schoch for Elector Friedrich IV, carries statues of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a>' ancestors by Sebastian Götz and holds the court chapel; it is the best preserved part of the castle.</p><p>Frederick V married Elizabeth Stuart, daughter of the English king, and brought the engineer Salomon de Caus to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, where he began an enormous garden on the mountain slope. Contemporaries regarded the earthworks it required as the eighth wonder of the world.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">Battle of White Mountain</a> in 1620 the Palatinate was left undefended. General Tilly took the town on 16 September 1622 and the castle a few days later. The Swedes captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> in 1633, imperial troops retook the castle in 1635, and the new elector Charles Louis moved into the ruined building on 7 October 1649. French troops destroyed much of it in 1689 and again in 1693, and a lightning strike in 1764 destroyed rebuilt sections.</p><p>Afterwards the ruins served as a quarry, and townsfolk took stone, wood and iron from them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/august-von-kotzebue">August von Kotzebue</a> protested in 1803 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>'s intention to pull them down. At the start of the 19th century the ruin became a symbol of the patriotic movement against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, and Romantic painters, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-m-w-turner">J. M. W. Turner</a> among them, painted it repeatedly. By 1880 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-twain">Mark Twain</a> mentioned it as a ruin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heidelberg Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Palatinate suffered again in the Nine Years' War, when Louis XIV's troops, pressing the inheritance claims of Elizabeth Charlotte, duchess of Orléans, systematically burned Heidelberg, Mannheim, Speyer and Worms in 1689, and <b>Heidelberg Castle</b> was blown up in 1689 and 1693.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Thirty Years' War, <b>Heidelberg Castle</b> surrendered to the Imperial-Spanish army of the Count of Tilly and Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba, three days after the city, capital of the Palatinate, was stormed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-19">19 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
